  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep potential vorticity troughing with phased upper level jet \ndynamics will push across Southeast Michigan early today. Forecasted \nheight fields support negative tilting of the axis as it arrives but \nwith the strongest 1000-500mb height falls passing to the north and \neast of the Upper Peninsula. A late timing of the deepening, \nrelative to Southeast Michigan, results in an extremely narrow \nribbon of moisture with shallow midlevel saturation.  Forecast \nsoundings support very dry air in the lowest 10.0 kft agl. Modestly \nbetter lapse rates above 12.0 kft agl resides over the northern cwa \nbetween 9-13z. The better potential to measure is north of the I 69 \ncorridor with much of the area at less than 40% to exceed a 6 hourly \nrate of 0.05 inches of liquid. Forecasted PoPs may be a little low \nfor the Tri Cities and Thumb but given the amount of dry air in the \nlow levels will maintain the inherited. Gradient flow with mixing \nwill result in a bump for surface winds this afternoon. West to \nnorthwest this afternoon of 15 to 25 mph. Highs today will be in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s, the true cold front not pushing through the \narea until this evening.\n\nSharp geopotential height rises over the Great Lakes region will \nsupport surface high pressure Monday and Tuesday. Much cooler \nweather is expected Monday with highs in the upper 50s to around 60 \ndespite full sunshine. Heart of the anticyclone will be overhead \nMonday night with dewpoints forecasted to drop into the middle 30s. \nWith a calm wind environment the likelihood is high that a Frost \nAdvisory will be needed Tuesday morning for at least a portion of \nthe forecast area.\n\nThe next upper level jet packet with a cyclonically curved exit \nregion will push into the Great Lakes late Tuesday and Wednesday. \nWill need to monitor the southward extent to the absolute vorticity \nadvection as it relates to the PoP forecast. The current forecast for \nthe probability of precipitation is low for the midweek period. The \nforecast area looks to be perilously close to troughing which offers \nuncertainty for PoPs heading into next weekend. Temperatures look to \nbe around normal next weekend.",
